{"name":"Emerson","hex":"#3e6058","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Emerson","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname originating as a patronymic.","A male given name transferred from the surname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Emerson"}],"entries":[{"title":"Emerson (surname)","blocks":[{"text":"Emerson is an English surname derived from Anglo-Saxon Emars sunu, meaning \"Emar's son\" or \"Ethelmar's son\". Another origin has been suggested as starting with the Old French epic hero Aimeri de Narbonne which passed into Italian as Amerigo and subsequently into English as Emery, Amery, and Imray, among others; Emerson is thought to derived as a patronymic from Emery."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerson_(surname)"},{"title":"Emerson (given name)","blocks":[{"text":"Emerson is an English given name, a transferred use of an English surname coming either from Anglo-Saxon Emars sunu, \"Emar's son,\" or from \"Emery's son.\" It is in use for both boys and girls. Spelling variants such as Emersyn are also in regular use."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerson_(given_name)"},{"title":"Emerson (footballer, born 1976)","blocks":[{"text":"Emerson Ferreira da Rosa, simply known as Emerson, is a Brazilian former professional footballer who played as a defensive midfielder. At international level, he played 73 games for the Brazil national team between 1997 and 2006, scoring 6 goals. With Brazil, he has taken part in two FIFA World Cups, two editions of the Copa América, and three Confederations Cups, winning the 1999 Copa América and the 2005 FIFA Confederations Cup, while also reaching the 1998 World Cup Final."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Emerson_2005.jpg/330px-Emerson_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerson_(footballer%2C_born_1976)"}],"images":[],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{